1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Cars that can sustain blasts of gunfire.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Glass that won't break.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
And people who are targeted to die.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,000
But don't.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
So how do you stop a speeding bullet?

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, Bulletproof on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,800
February 28, 1997.

8
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:43,420
A shootout at the Bank of America in North Hollywood, California.

9
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:48,480
Bank robbers dressed from head to toe in body armor storm the bank.

10
00:00:49,240 --> 00:00:53,480
As police surround the building, the robbers calmly walk out, machine guns blasting.

11
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:57,260
The handguns the police use can't penetrate their body armor,

12
00:00:57,261 --> 00:00:59,680
so the criminals casually make their getaway.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,900
Though it seems there's nothing police can do to bring them down,

14
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:09,040
the robbers are eventually mortally wounded after the police buy more powerful firearms at a local gun store.

15
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:16,160
But patrol officers did not have any weapon in the field that was able to penetrate that vest.

16
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:17,760
Now they do.

17
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:22,100
Ever since the North Hollywood shootout, they've given patrolmen better handguns

18
00:01:22,101 --> 00:01:25,180
so he can defeat the body armor that suspects were wearing.

19
00:01:25,181 --> 00:01:31,040
As the Bank of America's shootout and subsequent rearming of the Los Angeles Police Department illustrates,

20
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:35,100
the age-old duel between weapon and shield rages on.

21
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:41,760
Today's weapons, of course, are ballistic, and the shields are bulletproof.

22
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:48,820
In the midst of this covert world of top-secret technology and scientific testing

23
00:01:48,821 --> 00:01:53,280
lies an amazing array of materials which can stop even the most powerful bullet.

24
00:01:53,281 --> 00:01:58,280
The technology is so advanced, it is invisible.

25
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:06,280
Everywhere we look, though we may not see it, there is protection against gunfire.

26
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:11,280
We took our bullet-resistant fiberglass panels and introduced the application inside walls.

27
00:02:11,281 --> 00:02:14,280
And you never know which walls are bullet-resistant.

28
00:02:14,281 --> 00:02:19,280
All of our vehicles are made to look like a common vehicle.

29
00:02:19,281 --> 00:02:24,280
So an attacker would not be able to tell that the vehicle is armored.

30
00:02:24,281 --> 00:02:28,280
Sometimes the protection is made obvious so as to intimidate the enemy.

31
00:02:28,281 --> 00:02:33,280
Loomis Fargo and Company wants high profile vehicles.

32
00:02:33,281 --> 00:02:38,280
We want the bad guys to know that we need business.

33
00:02:38,281 --> 00:02:43,280
Whatever the choice, the decision to guard against the threat of a firearm is a complicated one.

34
00:02:43,281 --> 00:02:46,280
The potential danger must first be identified.

35
00:02:46,281 --> 00:02:51,760
It's important to sit down with the customer and find out exactly what they feel they need protection against.

36
00:02:51,761 --> 00:02:57,660
We actually call out levels of protection by the type of ammunition we're providing protection against.

37
00:02:58,380 --> 00:03:05,140
Since a given material may only stop certain types of bullets, most industry experts refer to them as bullet resistant.

38
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:10,200
For each benefit you find in a new material, it also has its Achilles heel.

39
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:14,040
And the key in all this is where do you hide the Achilles heel?

40
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:19,660
The biggest challenge, of course, is getting these remarkable materials to succeed in the real world.

41
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:23,580
Really, it's always been a trade-off between protection and mobility,

42
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:28,740
because as you try to increase the protection level to stop bigger and faster bullets,

43
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:30,300
you have to increase weight.

44
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:35,220
When you add weight to a vehicle, you have to increase the horsepower of the system.

45
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:36,860
A soldier gets tired.

46
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:41,020
As you increase the weight, it degrades the performance of the soldier in the field.

47
00:03:41,021 --> 00:03:48,020
The race to develop gear that can neutralize deadly weapons started with the beginning of warfare.

48
00:03:48,021 --> 00:03:57,020
After mastering metallurgy around 7000 BC, ancient cultures began fashioning both weapons and protection against them.

49
00:03:57,021 --> 00:04:01,020
The Greeks sophisticated the techniques.

50
00:04:01,021 --> 00:04:08,020
Once metalworking became widespread, bronze armor became very popular.

51
00:04:08,021 --> 00:04:14,140
Even when they developed iron for bladed weapons, they still kept bronze for body armor because it was supple.

52
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:17,360
Being soft, it would absorb a blow rather than shatter.

53
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:22,100
The Romans were particularly adept at working with several different forms of armor.

54
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:27,740
Leather works on the principle that it doesn't try to deflect a blow.

55
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:30,100
It tries to absorb it.

56
00:04:30,101 --> 00:04:39,140
mail, which is interlinked rings of iron, would work very well against a cutting blow.

57
00:04:39,141 --> 00:04:44,320
It was actually probably the most common form of armor worn by the Romans,

58
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:49,900
more familiar than the interlinked strips of armor, a lorica segmentata.

59
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:55,340
After the fall of Rome, armorers continued to experiment with body armor.

60
00:04:55,341 --> 00:05:00,520
but the breastplate and chainmail remained virtually unchanged until the late 14th century

61
00:05:00,521 --> 00:05:04,260
when full iron plate armor began to make its way onto the battlefield.

62
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:10,480
These suits took months to make and were so expensive only the elite,

63
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:13,100
the knights who rode with the army, could afford them.

64
00:05:13,980 --> 00:05:18,400
But they did provide an extraordinary degree of protection against the weapons of their time.

65
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:22,200
It really did start to heat up in the 14th century.

66
00:05:22,201 --> 00:05:29,260
With the introduction of plate armor, the weapons of the 13th century became far less effective.

67
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:34,080
The stabbing and hacking sword, the longbow, could not penetrate the plate.

68
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:38,400
The protection that plate armor provided would not last long.

69
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:43,280
The gun appeared on the battlefields of Europe around 1360.

70
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:48,400
In its earliest form, it was no more than a crude, handheld version of the cannon,

71
00:05:48,401 --> 00:05:51,120
but its potential for blasting through defenses.

72
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:53,260
was obvious.

73
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:57,940
The handgun upped the ante in this competition

74
00:05:57,941 --> 00:06:00,480
between the weapon maker and the armorer

75
00:06:00,481 --> 00:06:02,840
because with the chemical power of the bullet,

76
00:06:03,240 --> 00:06:06,740
you could get something that hit as hard as the most powerful crossbow.

77
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:08,720
It was a very simple, primitive weapon

78
00:06:08,721 --> 00:06:10,700
that could be issued in large numbers.

79
00:06:11,660 --> 00:06:13,660
By the late 1400s,

80
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:15,880
improvements in the firing mechanism of the gun

81
00:06:15,881 --> 00:06:18,020
made it much more accurate and popular.

82
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:21,680
The bullets soon began penetrating the iron plate armor.

83
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:26,860
It seemed the suits, only an eighth of an inch thick, would soon become obsolete.

84
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:32,740
But resourceful armorers developed a better protection by adding more carbon to the iron,

85
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:34,800
forming a rudimentary steel.

86
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:37,780
The breastplate was now twice as solid.

87
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:43,500
By the middle of the 16th century, the armorer had gotten a little ahead.

88
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:47,940
So it had once more gotten to the point where if you had enough money,

89
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:50,400
you could buy survival on the battlefield.

90
00:06:50,401 --> 00:06:56,400
Again, only well-to-do aristocratic knights on horseback were able to pay the price.

91
00:06:56,401 --> 00:07:02,400
This was the period when the term bulletproof came into the lexicon.

92
00:07:02,401 --> 00:07:07,400
In the 16th century, to prove was to test.

93
00:07:07,401 --> 00:07:15,400
And the armors would prove their plate armor by taking a pistol and shooting the pistol into the breastplate.

94
00:07:15,401 --> 00:07:17,400
And it would leave a little dent.

95
00:07:17,401 --> 00:07:21,620
and whenever someone was buying breastplates,

96
00:07:21,780 --> 00:07:23,000
they'd look for that little dent

97
00:07:23,001 --> 00:07:25,720
that proved that breastplate was bulletproof.

98
00:07:26,800 --> 00:07:29,520
Though a cavalryman was better protected with heavier armor,

99
00:07:30,120 --> 00:07:31,580
the protection came at a price.

100
00:07:32,180 --> 00:07:35,260
It further impeded his mobility and ability to fight.

101
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:37,500
They were slow,

102
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:43,740
and while they could manage a quick gallop for a charge,

103
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:47,000
the horse was the one who tired.

104
00:07:47,001 --> 00:07:50,040
The heavier the rider, the less the horse could do.

105
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:53,760
And these Lancers essentially had their one charge.

106
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:57,700
Then their horses would be spent, and they would be sitting ducks.

107
00:07:59,140 --> 00:08:03,320
In the late 1500s, the advantage once again shifted to gun makers,

108
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:07,440
who had designed a weapon with a more powerful bullet speed, the musket.

109
00:08:09,300 --> 00:08:13,020
The gun was almost 6 feet long, weighed about 20 pounds,

110
00:08:13,021 --> 00:08:16,640
and could fire a 75 caliber round more than 100 yards.

111
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:22,660
Because its large barrel was able to hold more gunpowder,

112
00:08:23,180 --> 00:08:26,820
by 1650 the musket could blast through even the heaviest armor.

113
00:08:27,740 --> 00:08:29,640
It was the anti-tank weapon of the period.

114
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:36,340
It upped the anti once again to the point where there was just no question.

115
00:08:36,341 --> 00:08:41,120
There was no plate armor around that could stop a musket.

116
00:08:41,121 --> 00:08:47,420
The key to firearms in this early era is you didn't need any skill.

117
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:51,180
Anybody could be taught to load a firearm.

118
00:08:51,700 --> 00:08:53,620
You didn't need constant practice like a longbow.

119
00:08:54,780 --> 00:08:59,000
As gun designers continued to make improvements in accuracy and firepower,

120
00:08:59,600 --> 00:09:02,360
European leaders began conscripting massive armies.

121
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:04,940
During this period, the focus was on firepower.

122
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:08,780
The commanders, who had a very scientific view of warfare,

123
00:09:08,781 --> 00:09:12,020
didn't want to distract from that purpose.

124
00:09:12,380 --> 00:09:16,560
So they tried to make the soldier a shooting machine.

125
00:09:17,140 --> 00:09:20,500
Armor was considered to be a distraction.

126
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:24,120
Guns were cheap, and so were men.

127
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:26,660
Armor had no place on the battlefield.

128
00:09:27,740 --> 00:09:32,520
The bullet would hold the upper hand in the race between weapons and protective gear for hundreds of years.

129
00:09:33,080 --> 00:09:36,880
It was only when soldiers found themselves trapped in trenches in World War I

130
00:09:36,881 --> 00:09:38,980
that a new form of body armor appeared.

131
00:09:40,900 --> 00:09:44,320
The way you kill a man in a trench

132
00:09:44,321 --> 00:09:48,220
is with an airburst of artillery.

133
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:52,420
You have a round shower of the trench with fragments.

134
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:55,880
This created considerable casualties among men

135
00:09:55,881 --> 00:09:59,260
whose only protection was a wool hat.

136
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:05,920
The helmet was invented by the French in 1915.

137
00:10:06,620 --> 00:10:09,120
It was soon adopted by the Americans in 1917.

138
00:10:10,060 --> 00:10:13,900
Made of steel, the helmet adequately protected soldiers from shrapnel bursts.

139
00:10:15,780 --> 00:10:21,960
Protective vests for the Allied soldiers were tested during the war by Englishman Sir Arthur Conan Doyle.

140
00:10:22,600 --> 00:10:30,400
But General Sir Douglas Hay, commander of the British forces, rejected the idea on the grounds that only cowards would want to wear one.

141
00:10:30,401 --> 00:10:36,060
Though several tests were conducted over the next few decades,

142
00:10:36,860 --> 00:10:40,660
body armor wasn't available to soldiers for the first half of the 20th century.

143
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:41,820
It was just too heavy.

144
00:10:43,140 --> 00:10:48,860
Survival on the 20th century battlefield is all about moving very fast

145
00:10:48,861 --> 00:10:55,020
and being able to drop down behind cover, and it's extremely tiring.

146
00:10:55,500 --> 00:11:00,120
You're going to be a sitting duck if you happen to have this full body protection.

147
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:06,300
They had all changed with the introduction of a new synthetic fiber created by DuPont in the 1930s.

148
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:07,900
Nylon.

149
00:11:08,580 --> 00:11:10,700
Much stronger than most natural fibers,

150
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:15,420
researchers discovered that by tightly weaving nylon, then building it up in layers,

151
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:17,800
it could resist some ammunition.

152
00:11:18,920 --> 00:11:21,600
The new material was quickly dubbed ballistic nylon.

153
00:11:24,220 --> 00:11:27,960
Flag jackets made of ballistic nylon appeared in the last years of World War II.

154
00:11:28,720 --> 00:11:32,240
They were used to keep airmen safe from the shrapnel of anti-aircraft guns.

155
00:11:33,020 --> 00:11:40,360
The infantryman, however, remained unprotected until an improved flak jacket became standard issue during the Korean War.

156
00:11:41,300 --> 00:11:45,100
It consisted of rigid plates made of aluminum and covered with ballistic nylon.

157
00:11:46,720 --> 00:11:50,100
By Vietnam, flak jackets had once again been improved.

158
00:11:51,380 --> 00:11:56,080
The military found that by adding more layers of ballistic nylon and removing the metal plates,

159
00:11:56,780 --> 00:11:58,420
the jackets afforded better protection.

160
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:03,920
These jackets were essentially the predecessors to the modern-day bulletproof vest,

161
00:12:04,420 --> 00:12:06,840
but they were still ineffective against a direct hit.

162
00:12:08,100 --> 00:12:09,756
In the early 1970s, the U.S.

163
00:12:09,757 --> 00:12:13,600
Army began testing several tougher materials in body armor vests.

164
00:12:14,760 --> 00:12:17,140
At the same time, civilian Richard Davis,

165
00:12:17,141 --> 00:12:19,900
who had been shot by a robber while delivering pizza,

166
00:12:20,580 --> 00:12:25,580
began experimenting with a super-strong plastic called Kevlar, produced by DuPont.

167
00:12:26,080 --> 00:12:31,060
The new material was then being used to strengthen tires.

168
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:35,580
But Davis put the material in a vest and used himself as the test subject.

169
00:12:35,581 --> 00:12:41,100
Quite frankly, the officers did not believe that this type of soft material could stop bullets.

170
00:12:42,160 --> 00:12:47,060
At that point, he decided to get in front of a large group of police officers in the Detroit area,

171
00:12:47,580 --> 00:12:48,980
where he did shoot himself.

172
00:12:50,220 --> 00:12:53,140
Davis not only wanted to prove he could survive a direct hit,

173
00:12:53,141 --> 00:12:56,340
but that he could fire his gun immediately after an attack.

174
00:13:23,140 --> 00:13:30,140
the men in the next 10 years. If it doesn't, they're going to die as I will.

175
00:13:42,140 --> 00:13:49,140
Okay. It stings. It stings. It's enough to make you mad.

176
00:13:49,141 --> 00:13:58,260
The daring all-or-nothing experiment was a success.

177
00:14:01,020 --> 00:14:06,560
Today, Davis' company, Second Chance, is the largest supplier of body armor in North America.

178
00:14:07,420 --> 00:14:11,000
The material for Second Chance vests comes in bulk to the manufacturer,

179
00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:14,280
where it is assembled into multiple layers and compressed.

180
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:17,660
Each layer of material has a specific function.

181
00:14:18,460 --> 00:14:21,620
The inner layers absorb moisture from the body and help the vest breathe.

182
00:14:22,300 --> 00:14:26,060
The outer layer is usually a ballistics nylon that helps slow the bullet down.

183
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:32,340
The middle layer, which is bullet resistant, is usually made of Kevlar, a tightly woven rigid polymer.

184
00:14:34,040 --> 00:14:36,720
Its function is to stop a bullet from entering the vest.

185
00:14:37,540 --> 00:14:41,240
Because manufacturers strive to design lightweight, comfortable vests,

186
00:14:41,241 --> 00:14:44,660
sometimes the material's ability to resist bullets is compromised.

187
00:14:44,661 --> 00:14:50,380
In fact, almost 50% of body armor vests fail to stop around in the testing phase.

188
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:55,040
And so scientists must continually try to develop better materials and designs.

189
00:14:57,700 --> 00:15:00,440
Up next, the science of stopping a bullet.

190
00:15:02,700 --> 00:15:09,380
The saying, a flash in the pan, originated when gunpowder in the firing mechanism of a musket rifle was ignited

191
00:15:09,381 --> 00:15:12,420
and quickly burned without launching the bullet.

192
00:15:12,421 --> 00:15:15,420
Bulletproof will return on Modern Marvels.

193
00:15:17,420 --> 00:15:20,420
We now return to Bulletproof on Modern Marvels.

194
00:15:22,420 --> 00:15:23,420
Please don't move!

195
00:15:24,420 --> 00:15:27,420
To stop a bullet from killing a person takes some pretty amazing science.

196
00:15:32,420 --> 00:15:37,420
Theoretically, you could put a tombstone around someone's neck if they could carry it.

197
00:15:37,421 --> 00:15:42,420
if they could carry it and would offer a maximum amount of protection against a number of small arms.

198
00:15:42,421 --> 00:15:45,420
A tombstone, however, would be extremely impractical.

199
00:15:45,421 --> 00:15:50,420
Enter science and technology and a tough rating system to make sure that everything works.

200
00:15:50,421 --> 00:15:58,420
Kevlar, produced by DuPont, is effective because it stops a bullet by disseminating the energy over a wider area.

201
00:15:58,421 --> 00:16:03,420
Much like a baseball mitt absorbing the impact of a baseball,

202
00:16:03,421 --> 00:16:06,040
The person wearing the Kevlar becomes the catcher.

203
00:16:08,900 --> 00:16:14,060
At Southwest Research Institute, the Material Development Department, Ballistics Division,

204
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:17,120
conducts tests on all types of ballistic materials.

205
00:16:17,480 --> 00:16:20,640
The materials are usually brought to the small range facility for testing.

206
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:25,400
This is the universal gun system that we use to fire the small arms projectiles in this lab.

207
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:27,480
Here's where we measure our projectile velocity.

208
00:16:27,880 --> 00:16:29,180
These are chronograph screens.

209
00:16:29,181 --> 00:16:34,660
They have a series of infrared emitters at the top and a series of infrared detectors here at the bottom.

210
00:16:34,940 --> 00:16:36,900
It makes a plane of infrared light.

211
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:40,580
When the projectile passes through, it interferes with some of that light,

212
00:16:40,680 --> 00:16:43,280
which is detected by the electronics of this,

213
00:16:43,620 --> 00:16:47,560
and it sends a signal into the other room to a time interval counter.

214
00:16:48,180 --> 00:16:53,340
Based on the known distance between those two screens and the known time between those two screens,

215
00:16:53,420 --> 00:16:54,400
we can calculate the velocity.

216
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:58,040
Just beyond the velocity measurement system, we have the target area.

217
00:16:58,041 --> 00:17:01,880
The instrumentation that we're going to be using today is the Emicon 468.

218
00:17:01,881 --> 00:17:06,500
It's an ultra high-speed digital camera system and this camera system will take

219
00:17:06,501 --> 00:17:10,800
a picture every 10 nanoseconds and if you do the math on that, that's a hundred

220
00:17:10,801 --> 00:17:14,780
million frames per second and we only get eight pictures out of it but we get

221
00:17:14,781 --> 00:17:19,460
them very quickly. The data gathered here will be sent to a ballistic

222
00:17:19,461 --> 00:17:24,200
scientist who will study and evaluate the information for the client. My role

223
00:17:24,201 --> 00:17:30,620
in Ballistics Research is looking at the ballistic event from a numerical simulation point of view.

224
00:17:30,840 --> 00:17:32,620
We do large-scale computations.

225
00:17:32,621 --> 00:17:36,180
The first simulation shows a round hitting a hard steel plate,

226
00:17:36,300 --> 00:17:38,729
and this is a ball round. It's filled with lead, as you can see.

227
00:17:38,730 --> 00:17:39,640
That's the yellow color.

228
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:44,600
And as the round comes down and hits the hard steel plate, you'll see that it begins to deform a lot.

229
00:17:44,860 --> 00:17:47,700
That's because the steel plate is strong and it doesn't want to bend.

230
00:17:48,080 --> 00:17:52,380
But the bullet's moving very fast, and it continues moving even though the front has stopped.

231
00:17:52,381 --> 00:17:57,380
The Institute also studies what happens when a material doesn't stop a bullet.

232
00:17:57,381 --> 00:18:02,380
Here we design new armors and because of that a lot of our tests you actually have a perforation of the armor.

233
00:18:02,381 --> 00:18:08,380
The bullet goes through because we're really pushing the limit on how light we can go and new designs, ideas like that.

234
00:18:08,381 --> 00:18:10,380
Because of that when we run a test we compare those things.

235
00:18:10,381 --> 00:18:14,683
Like what happened inside that target plate? Why did it end up failing?

236
00:18:14,684 --> 00:18:16,380
Where did the failure start?

237
00:18:16,381 --> 00:18:21,380
The Southwest Research Institute is also studying a promising new class of materials.

238
00:18:21,381 --> 00:18:27,380
high-tolerance ceramics, which don't shatter, but as of now are too heavy for most situations.

239
00:18:27,381 --> 00:18:32,380
We also did a test where a hard ceramic element completely defeat an armor-piercing round.

240
00:18:32,381 --> 00:18:35,380
Ceramics are very hard, and as long as they can be kept intact,

241
00:18:35,381 --> 00:18:40,380
they're able to completely defeat a round without any deformation or failure of the target.

242
00:18:40,381 --> 00:18:46,380
And as we look at that kind of information, we're able to build up a complete picture of what happened to this armor,

243
00:18:46,381 --> 00:18:50,380
and through that, we're able to design a better armor, one which will stop the round.

244
00:18:51,380 --> 00:18:56,280
When the test results are in, the information from labs like these is sent to the government.

245
00:18:57,680 --> 00:19:02,420
A rating system has been established to certify that body armor meets certain standards.

246
00:19:03,320 --> 00:19:10,180
The program is under the jurisdiction of the National Institute of Justice, or NIJ, governed by the Department of Justice.

247
00:19:10,600 --> 00:19:16,780
If it passes, it comes back to us and we issue the certification documents, which goes back to the manufacturer,

248
00:19:17,280 --> 00:19:20,860
and we put them in our database so we can attest that they have, in fact, passed.

249
00:19:21,380 --> 00:19:24,600
Testing for military protective gear proceeds quite differently.

250
00:19:25,840 --> 00:19:29,320
A company that makes a product for the government must follow strict guidelines.

251
00:19:30,460 --> 00:19:36,000
Ceridine, located in Southern California, produces a large portion of the military's hard body armor.

252
00:19:36,001 --> 00:19:49,500
The military has to define their requirements based on their experience

253
00:19:49,501 --> 00:19:56,520
as to what various soldiers or equipment will find in a battlefield condition.

254
00:19:57,100 --> 00:19:59,140
And that's a very wide range.

255
00:19:59,660 --> 00:20:02,920
Ceridine's products, based on advanced technical ceramics,

256
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:05,160
are at the pinnacle of bulletproof materials.

257
00:20:05,161 --> 00:20:13,560
The idea of a ceramic stopping a projectile may be quite alien to the normal way of thinking.

258
00:20:14,180 --> 00:20:17,880
We all know that if we drop a plate, it breaks.

259
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:24,400
But there are ways of putting a backing on the ceramic to keep those cracks from propagating.

260
00:20:25,420 --> 00:20:32,060
Ceridine produces a man-made, lightweight, extremely dense ceramic created of boron carbide.

261
00:20:32,061 --> 00:20:36,360
Boron carbide is one of the hardest synthetic substances known.

262
00:20:36,361 --> 00:20:42,060
It is exceeded in hardness only by a diamond.

263
00:20:42,061 --> 00:20:46,860
Because the raw materials are formed and pressed under heat, the process starts in Ceridine's

264
00:20:46,861 --> 00:20:47,860
foundry.

265
00:20:47,861 --> 00:20:51,400
The process initially starts with the powder.

266
00:20:51,401 --> 00:20:58,060
That powder is then formed into a preform in a high pressure press.

267
00:20:58,061 --> 00:21:05,840
We take the preform of the ceramic and then place it into an electrically heated induction

268
00:21:05,841 --> 00:21:12,280
furnace, usually under an inert atmosphere of nitrogen or argon.

269
00:21:12,281 --> 00:21:19,760
The reason for that, of course, is to stop the tooling and even the ceramic from oxidizing.

270
00:21:19,761 --> 00:21:23,580
The furnace is heated up in excess of 2,000 degrees centigrade.

271
00:21:23,581 --> 00:21:25,840
The ceramic is then cleaned.

272
00:21:25,841 --> 00:21:29,800
We use polymers to put the backing on.

273
00:21:29,801 --> 00:21:38,040
The polymers made out of pevlar are then bonded with resin and then compressed onto the back

274
00:21:38,041 --> 00:21:45,600
face of the ceramic in order to ensure that once the projectile has hit the ceramic and

275
00:21:45,601 --> 00:21:48,440
broken, it doesn't go any further.

276
00:21:48,441 --> 00:21:54,460
Remarkably, materials more brittle than ceramic have been stopping bullets for decades.

277
00:21:56,760 --> 00:22:02,300
Glass, the most fragile of building materials, so breakable even a pebble can shatter it.

278
00:22:03,180 --> 00:22:07,360
But when glass is combined with a thin layer of polycarbonate made from plastics,

279
00:22:08,220 --> 00:22:09,900
it can effectively stop a bullet.

280
00:22:10,320 --> 00:22:12,760
It too becomes essentially transparent armor.

281
00:22:12,761 --> 00:22:18,100
You need multiple sheets because you don't want the fractures which start on one side to run all the way to the other.

282
00:22:18,440 --> 00:22:22,580
So we have an impact happening and we want to try to localize the damage to the glass

283
00:22:22,581 --> 00:22:26,480
so that it actually takes many layers, but it doesn't break all the way through.

284
00:22:28,080 --> 00:22:31,440
Laminated glass was invented for use in autos in 1909.

285
00:22:32,080 --> 00:22:36,140
It consisted of several layers of thick glass with thin, clear plastic in between.

286
00:22:36,940 --> 00:22:38,920
The layers were bonded together by heat.

287
00:22:39,880 --> 00:22:42,960
The glass was used in airplane windshields during World War I

288
00:22:42,961 --> 00:22:46,760
and by the 1920s in police vans and armored cars.

289
00:22:48,440 --> 00:22:52,000
But it wasn't just the cops who used bulletproof glass.

290
00:22:52,680 --> 00:22:57,220
Wealthy criminals also paid to have the glass installed in their cars for protection from the police.

291
00:22:57,940 --> 00:22:59,680
The armor had one flaw, though.

292
00:23:00,220 --> 00:23:03,160
The car was easy to spot because the windshields were flat.

293
00:23:03,880 --> 00:23:08,000
The technology to curve bulletproof glass wouldn't make its appearance for another decade.

294
00:23:09,500 --> 00:23:15,780
In the 1950s, a process was developed to bend laminated glass for use in car and plane windshields.

295
00:23:15,781 --> 00:23:22,960
By the 1970s, it was discovered that thinner layers of glass would flex more before they broke.

296
00:23:24,240 --> 00:23:32,900
That decade, DuPont created Spall Shield, a distinctly improved bulletproof glass for buildings such as hospitals, storm shelters, and schools.

297
00:23:33,940 --> 00:23:38,960
The glass prevents spalling, the shower of glass that occurs when conventional glass shatters,

298
00:23:39,860 --> 00:23:45,600
and can stop various levels of penetration, including high-powered gunfire and explosions.

299
00:23:45,780 --> 00:23:53,020
in the last few decades with crime and street violence on the rise

300
00:23:53,021 --> 00:23:58,160
bulletproof polycarbonate glass created completely from plastics has made an appearance

301
00:23:58,161 --> 00:24:02,180
this glass can crush and absorb a bullet upon impact

302
00:24:02,181 --> 00:24:07,720
the polycarbonate is less expensive than real glass with plastic layers

303
00:24:07,721 --> 00:24:09,720
but it is harder to see through

304
00:24:09,721 --> 00:24:14,720
Safeguard Security Company in Texas specializes in polycarbonate products

305
00:24:14,721 --> 00:24:19,820
for companies with security concerns, such as banks, fast food restaurants, and gas stations.

306
00:24:20,600 --> 00:24:24,000
Our standard window has a bullet-resistant dip tray in the window

307
00:24:24,001 --> 00:24:27,680
that allows people to make transactions from a safe side to a non-safe side.

308
00:24:28,280 --> 00:24:31,980
We also make bullet-resistant transactions that have transparent hopper devices

309
00:24:31,981 --> 00:24:36,040
that allow you to actually see bigger packages transferred from one side of the window to the other.

310
00:24:37,540 --> 00:24:40,900
Though it's sometimes difficult to detect bullet-resistant glass,

311
00:24:41,720 --> 00:24:47,220
It's virtually impossible to know whether a material similar to Kevlar has been hidden inside walls.

312
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:54,780
Armotex, a division of Safeguard Security, fortifies walls and doors with a lightweight bullet-resistant barrier.

313
00:24:56,300 --> 00:25:01,900
Bullet-resistant fiberglass was introduced into the industry because it needed a new option for bullet-resistant protection.

314
00:25:02,600 --> 00:25:06,900
At that time, there were only two options, bullet-resistant Kevlar and bullet-resistant steel.

315
00:25:06,901 --> 00:25:12,900
Bullet resistant Kevlar is too expensive and bullet resistant steel was very difficult to install and it was very heavy.

316
00:25:12,901 --> 00:25:15,900
Our product is very nice because it's very light and it's easy to install.

317
00:25:15,901 --> 00:25:21,900
This is where the bullet actually penetrates here and the panel is designed so that the resin and the glass will separate at impact.

318
00:25:21,901 --> 00:25:24,900
And as it does, it absorbs the projectile here.

319
00:25:24,901 --> 00:25:30,900
Safeguard Security houses the complete manufacturing process for these super tough panels.

320
00:25:30,901 --> 00:25:35,900
Textile looms weave together the starch oil ballistic grade fiber.

321
00:25:35,901 --> 00:25:41,220
We start with the fabrics in our own textile operation, and we take a special woven roving that we've designed.

322
00:25:41,480 --> 00:25:46,140
It's got a special amount of yarns per inch of material that allow us to,

323
00:25:46,460 --> 00:25:50,320
in conjunction with bolt-resistant resins that we use, to make a flat, rigid panel.

324
00:25:51,240 --> 00:25:55,080
When finished, the panels are sold separately to reinforce walls

325
00:25:55,081 --> 00:25:59,000
and installed into metal doors that are also manufactured on site.

326
00:25:59,280 --> 00:26:03,280
What we do there is we take a metal skin, we put the fiberglass in the core of the door,

327
00:26:03,281 --> 00:26:07,500
but we put another skin over that and we weld it closed and make a hollow metal door.

328
00:26:11,320 --> 00:26:12,960
Wooden doors are fortified too.

329
00:26:13,500 --> 00:26:16,100
They are designed to look like any ordinary wooden door.

330
00:26:16,580 --> 00:26:18,140
Would-be criminals wouldn't know the difference.

331
00:26:18,400 --> 00:26:22,140
We take our bullet-resistant fiberglass panel and we adhere wood on both sides.

332
00:26:22,600 --> 00:26:24,520
Then we take a wood veneer and adhere it to that.

333
00:26:25,040 --> 00:26:30,200
Then we take that compound and we put it into a press to make sure it's perfectly flat

334
00:26:30,201 --> 00:26:33,660
and that the laminate is securely fastened to the door.

335
00:26:34,060 --> 00:26:37,100
Then we take it out of the press and we install it on the frame.

336
00:26:38,580 --> 00:26:41,760
While criminals may not know if a wall or door will stop a bullet,

337
00:26:42,480 --> 00:26:46,300
the sight of an armored truck is deliberately designed to leave no doubt.

338
00:26:47,080 --> 00:26:49,300
Up next, armor takes to the road.

339
00:26:51,740 --> 00:26:54,380
Bulletproof glass can withstand a round of gunfire

340
00:26:54,381 --> 00:26:58,640
exerting well over 150,000 pounds of pressure per square inch.

341
00:26:59,420 --> 00:27:01,720
Bulletproof will return on Modern Marvels.

342
00:27:06,300 --> 00:27:12,340
In the business of bulletproofing, not letting the enemy know what has been armored gives the defender a distinct advantage.

343
00:27:13,140 --> 00:27:17,340
But with armored trucks, the idea is to design them to look as impenetrable as possible.

344
00:27:17,341 --> 00:27:31,340
SWAT teams rely on highly fortified, highly intimidating vans called personnel carriers.

345
00:27:31,341 --> 00:27:37,340
In the old days, we might have to run up with a police car and position that in between us so we could rescue the victim.

346
00:27:37,341 --> 00:27:42,340
The guys that were driving that car or the policemen that were doing that rescue were in great danger.

347
00:27:42,341 --> 00:27:47,280
Now at least we have the capability of coming up with a ballistic vehicle

348
00:27:47,281 --> 00:27:50,560
that'll stop a lot of the rounds that the suspects are shooting.

349
00:27:51,080 --> 00:27:54,560
Inside, it's got very small windshields

350
00:27:54,561 --> 00:27:58,220
because they want the majority of the vehicle to be steel.

351
00:27:58,620 --> 00:28:01,840
It actually has a shield that pops up and protects somebody

352
00:28:01,841 --> 00:28:04,520
who can be up inside that hole with a gun,

353
00:28:04,620 --> 00:28:06,920
providing cover, fire, whatever we might need him to do.

354
00:28:07,740 --> 00:28:11,220
It's a very heavy vehicle. It weighs about 10,000 pounds.

355
00:28:11,221 --> 00:28:21,280
The back of the vehicle has two bench seats on either side, which can accommodate approximately six uniformed SWAT officers in the back.

356
00:28:22,240 --> 00:28:26,180
Humans are not the only cargo transported by these moving strongholds.

357
00:28:26,720 --> 00:28:28,460
Money is by far the largest haul.

358
00:28:29,700 --> 00:28:34,212
Loomis, one of America's biggest money haulers, moves massive amounts of currency for the U.S.

359
00:28:34,213 --> 00:28:34,913
government.

360
00:28:34,914 --> 00:28:40,520
We basically pick up the money at the Federal Reserve, take it to correspondent banks,

361
00:28:40,960 --> 00:28:45,380
and in turn, correspondence banks will then send it to commercial banks.

362
00:28:45,920 --> 00:28:48,600
Commercial banks will then send it to retail outlets.

363
00:28:48,960 --> 00:28:53,240
The company also transports money to and from automated tellers around the country.

364
00:28:54,600 --> 00:29:00,440
The imposing trucks, which weigh over 60,000 pounds, are armored throughout with several layers of metal

365
00:29:00,441 --> 00:29:03,620
and can withstand multiple high-powered gunfire.

366
00:29:04,740 --> 00:29:09,740
Remarkably, these armored vehicles are handmade.

367
00:29:09,741 --> 00:29:14,680
At the Supreme Manufacturing Facility near Fort Worth, Texas, it takes a team of craftsmen

368
00:29:14,681 --> 00:29:17,300
about 30 days to complete a cab from the ground up.

369
00:29:17,301 --> 00:29:20,740
To build an armored truck, we order in the material.

370
00:29:20,741 --> 00:29:26,380
We bring it together, cut it to size, and then we lay it out on what we call a jig.

371
00:29:26,381 --> 00:29:30,220
And we fully weld the armored truck inside and out.

372
00:29:30,221 --> 00:29:33,560
Surprisingly, the walls used to create the frame are made of aluminum.

373
00:29:35,380 --> 00:29:40,060
Heavily fortified aluminum absorbs the blow of a bullet by deforming, but not splitting.

374
00:29:40,920 --> 00:29:43,820
Once the frame is built, it is put onto the chassis and painted.

375
00:29:44,480 --> 00:29:47,040
It's now ready to protect our most valued assets.

376
00:29:48,580 --> 00:29:51,540
The progenitor of the armored truck, of course, is the tank,

377
00:29:51,940 --> 00:29:54,240
which made its first appearance during World War I.

378
00:29:55,340 --> 00:29:58,260
With two sides facing off against each other from trenches,

379
00:29:58,261 --> 00:30:01,400
generals needed to protect soldiers crossing enemy lines.

380
00:30:02,080 --> 00:30:03,120
The tank was the answer.

381
00:30:04,480 --> 00:30:07,520
By virtue of its thick steel plates, the tank was bulletproof,

382
00:30:07,780 --> 00:30:11,040
and it quickly transformed the landscape of modern warfare.

383
00:30:12,600 --> 00:30:16,820
Bulletproof transportation for commercial purposes immediately caught on after the war.

384
00:30:17,420 --> 00:30:20,380
In the post-war boom years, businesses were rapidly expanding,

385
00:30:20,800 --> 00:30:23,740
and transporting large sums of cash became problematic.

386
00:30:24,780 --> 00:30:27,240
Dunbar Armored, among others, helped fill the void.

387
00:30:27,241 --> 00:30:31,540
Back in the 20s, people were not paid by check.

388
00:30:31,960 --> 00:30:34,340
You were paid in cash from the factory.

389
00:30:35,180 --> 00:30:40,200
So the paymaster that worked at a company would have to physically go,

390
00:30:40,820 --> 00:30:45,480
get the payroll for that week and drive back to the factory.

391
00:30:45,680 --> 00:30:47,460
And that's where the holdups would take place.

392
00:30:47,660 --> 00:30:50,500
The manufacturers found that it was a lot easier

393
00:30:50,501 --> 00:30:55,420
just having the armored car pick up the money and deliver to the factories.

394
00:30:56,140 --> 00:30:59,920
But criminals soon had their cars specially reinforced with steel panels.

395
00:31:00,820 --> 00:31:04,260
The police were forced to follow suit by proofing their own personnel carriers

396
00:31:04,261 --> 00:31:06,060
to protect themselves from criminals.

397
00:31:07,780 --> 00:31:10,780
Hitler had a bulletproof car designed for him, too,

398
00:31:11,400 --> 00:31:13,700
though no one took aim at the car during World War II.

399
00:31:14,540 --> 00:31:18,360
When the American 101st Airborne Division finally made their way to Hitler's bunker,

400
00:31:19,120 --> 00:31:22,880
they riddled the car with bullets to vent their feelings about its former passenger.

401
00:31:25,420 --> 00:31:28,920
After World War II, America experienced another economic boom.

402
00:31:29,260 --> 00:31:33,800
All of a sudden there were banks that had branches out in the suburbs

403
00:31:33,801 --> 00:31:37,300
because the people were all moving out into the suburbs.

404
00:31:37,301 --> 00:31:41,640
That's when the armored car companies started servicing the branches.

405
00:31:42,440 --> 00:31:45,700
When automated tellers sprang up in the early 1980s,

406
00:31:46,040 --> 00:31:49,360
the armored truck business once again enjoyed an upsurge in demand.

407
00:31:50,440 --> 00:31:54,300
Today, Dunbar is one of the top three armored truck companies in the U.S.,

408
00:31:54,301 --> 00:31:58,300
hauling vast amounts of money for the government and the private sector.

409
00:31:58,301 --> 00:32:04,300
As with body armor, mobility versus protection is a key issue for armored trucks.

410
00:32:04,301 --> 00:32:08,300
How heavy can you make a truck and its payload and still find an engine to run it?

411
00:32:08,301 --> 00:32:17,300
You want to keep the weight down on the vehicle in order to be able to put more weight in coinage and so forth that you are carrying.

412
00:32:17,301 --> 00:32:23,300
An armored truck built today, the body of it will last for 15 years.

413
00:32:23,301 --> 00:32:30,920
The chassis won't last that long, so you're removing the body maybe three times to three different chassis.

414
00:32:31,580 --> 00:32:36,380
But the need for armored vehicles extends far beyond commercial and law enforcement use.

415
00:32:40,360 --> 00:32:44,580
Up next, arming a passenger vehicle against an attack.

416
00:32:44,581 --> 00:32:49,075
In 1903, a story called The Land Ironclads by H.G.

417
00:32:49,076 --> 00:32:54,200
Wells featured a self-propelled armored military vehicle.

418
00:32:55,060 --> 00:32:58,320
Thirteen years before the first tank appeared on the battlefield.

419
00:32:59,980 --> 00:33:02,160
Bulletproof will return on Modern Marvels.

420
00:33:05,680 --> 00:33:10,500
The news is full of frightening scenarios conjured by one-word headlines.

421
00:33:10,501 --> 00:33:18,960
ambushes attacks kidnappings hostages ransoms since the 1970s the rise in

422
00:33:18,961 --> 00:33:23,500
terrorism has forced executives celebrities and dignitaries to make their

423
00:33:23,501 --> 00:33:27,960
cars safe for travel this has created a lucrative niche for those in the business

424
00:33:27,961 --> 00:33:33,300
of providing armor-proofing materials Texas Armoring in San Antonio Texas

425
00:33:33,301 --> 00:33:37,740
fortifies passenger vehicles for a range of situations and threat levels around

426
00:33:37,741 --> 00:33:41,466
the world. In the United States, the market is growing fast.

427
00:33:41,467 --> 00:33:43,080
Most of the clients in the

428
00:33:43,081 --> 00:33:48,860
United States have been political figures or religious leaders. As always, balancing

429
00:33:48,861 --> 00:33:51,899
protection against mobility is the critical challenge.

430
00:33:51,900 --> 00:33:53,700
How to armor a car against heavy

431
00:33:53,701 --> 00:33:58,321
gunfire and still have it drive with the ease and maneuverability of a regular car?

432
00:33:58,322 --> 00:33:59,100
You are adding

433
00:33:59,101 --> 00:34:01,699
1,500 or 2,000 pounds to a vehicle.

434
00:34:01,700 --> 00:34:06,080
The weight added to the vehicle would prevent problems with

435
00:34:06,081 --> 00:34:12,180
the suspension, the braking system, but a lot of those we correct when we armor a vehicle.

436
00:34:12,660 --> 00:34:16,740
Armorers today are always searching for lightweight materials to lessen the load.

437
00:34:17,700 --> 00:34:21,960
SpectraShield, a new super light ballistics material, is used in the doors of the vehicles.

438
00:34:22,760 --> 00:34:25,560
Similar to Kevlar, SpectraShield has multiple layers

439
00:34:25,561 --> 00:34:29,440
and is pressed with a resin to form a hard bullet-resistant panel.

440
00:34:30,720 --> 00:34:34,220
To armor the door, we would take off the interior door panel

441
00:34:34,221 --> 00:34:37,700
and then put spectra shield into the door.

442
00:34:38,540 --> 00:34:41,680
And then when there's not a space where the spectra shield can fit

443
00:34:41,681 --> 00:34:43,320
because of the thickness of the spectra shield,

444
00:34:43,600 --> 00:34:45,300
we would replace it with ballistic steel.

445
00:34:45,580 --> 00:34:47,700
Then we would replace the door panel,

446
00:34:47,780 --> 00:34:49,760
and all the wiring basically stays the same.

447
00:34:49,761 --> 00:34:52,840
Then the window would be placed in and it would be fixed.

448
00:34:53,820 --> 00:34:57,400
The process is repeated throughout the car with various materials.

449
00:34:57,401 --> 00:35:07,180
When we armor a vehicle, the entire passenger compartment, which is the whole cabin area

450
00:35:07,181 --> 00:35:15,460
of the vehicle, will be protected, as well as the gas tank, the battery, the radiator,

451
00:35:15,461 --> 00:35:19,720
which would allow a vehicle more time to get out of the situation.

452
00:35:19,721 --> 00:35:23,040
The floor and ceiling of the cab is lined with thick ballistic nylon.

453
00:35:23,041 --> 00:35:31,280
The high hardened steel is the material we use in the firewall and in the pillars and

454
00:35:31,281 --> 00:35:38,440
posts everywhere that needs to be protected against direct hits of high power rifle.

455
00:35:38,441 --> 00:35:42,420
When finished, these cars look as if they're straight off the showroom floor, but they're

456
00:35:42,421 --> 00:35:44,900
as safe a fortress as the armored truck.

457
00:35:44,901 --> 00:35:50,320
An attacker would not be able to tell that the vehicle is armored.

458
00:35:50,321 --> 00:36:00,128
O'Gara Hess and Eisenhardt, the world's largest passenger vehicle armoring company, designed the first presidential bulletproof car at the behest of the Secret Service

459
00:36:00,129 --> 00:36:02,360
for President Harry S. Truman in 1948.

460
00:36:03,440 --> 00:36:06,160
They have fortified each presidential limousine since then.

461
00:36:07,140 --> 00:36:10,820
Like Texas Armoring, their clients fear terrorist threats as well.

462
00:36:13,460 --> 00:36:18,160
O'Gara Hess and Eisenhardt also beefs up many luxury cars for the private sector.

463
00:36:18,161 --> 00:36:21,400
and they armor vehicles for the military.

464
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:28,191
We are the sole source provider of the up-armored Humvee for the U.S.

465
00:36:28,192 --> 00:36:29,620
Army and Air Force.

466
00:36:30,340 --> 00:36:37,240
And this is a wheeled armored vehicle that's capable of defeating assault rifle threats and landmine attacks.

467
00:36:38,020 --> 00:36:43,500
As with armored trucks, aluminum is used to fortify O'Gara Hess and Eisenhart's passenger vehicles.

468
00:36:45,660 --> 00:36:47,600
The material speaks for itself.

469
00:36:48,160 --> 00:36:56,220
Every client who has been attacked by gunfire while driving O'Gara Hess and Eisenhart's vehicles has survived.

470
00:37:12,583 --> 00:37:18,480
Some clients in the armored car market require the ability to shoot back at an attacker.

471
00:37:18,481 --> 00:37:28,160
Ibis Tech in Pennsylvania has created a remarkable bulletproof vehicle with a remote-controlled large-caliber gun that can do just that.

472
00:37:28,960 --> 00:37:34,400
The company produces about 100 trucks a year with an asking price of up to $500,000.

473
00:37:35,020 --> 00:37:43,760
Ibis Tech has developed the Cobra Viper in response to known threats in particularly the Middle East.

474
00:37:43,761 --> 00:37:49,300
Our objective was to provide security and protection, which does not look offensive in nature.

475
00:37:50,300 --> 00:37:53,180
The gun is controlled from the passenger side of the vehicle.

476
00:37:54,020 --> 00:37:55,608
It is fully stabilized.

477
00:37:55,609 --> 00:37:59,820
It has gyroscopes that determine the movement of the vehicle,

478
00:37:59,940 --> 00:38:04,280
the movement of the weapon as it's being fired, so that it stabilizes the system,

479
00:38:04,560 --> 00:38:08,520
so that you get very accurate fire, even on the move.

480
00:38:09,460 --> 00:38:12,320
The high-caliber machine gun can strike a target.

481
00:38:12,321 --> 00:38:15,860
Viper commencing fire in 3, 2, 1.

482
00:38:18,220 --> 00:38:20,320
Over 1,000 yards away.

483
00:38:21,680 --> 00:38:28,020
Though one car company offered to install a machine gun in a bulletproof car they were fortifying for Pope John Paul II,

484
00:38:28,760 --> 00:38:30,280
the Vatican respectfully declined.

485
00:38:30,980 --> 00:38:34,900
It was decided it wouldn't look good to see the Pope blasting himself out of harm's way.

486
00:38:35,880 --> 00:38:38,440
The Pope has a fleet of over 20 different vehicles.

487
00:38:38,441 --> 00:38:45,380
They can be as unpretentious as an SUV, or as conspicuous as the car everyone associates him with, the Popemobile.

488
00:38:46,080 --> 00:38:49,480
The Pope has been riding in these bulletproof vehicles since 1981.

489
00:38:53,400 --> 00:38:58,280
Religious figures and other dignitaries, of course, also need to be protected outside their vehicles.

490
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:03,820
Up next, how to protect a politician from an assassin's bullet.

491
00:39:03,821 --> 00:39:10,180
The armoring of a passenger car can cost up to $100,000,

492
00:39:10,840 --> 00:39:12,920
not including the cost of the original vehicle.

493
00:39:13,820 --> 00:39:15,820
Bulletproof will return on Modern Marvels.

494
00:39:20,340 --> 00:39:23,280
In the race to keep ahead of ever-improving weapons,

495
00:39:24,260 --> 00:39:27,580
today's market is rife with emerging bullet-resistant technologies,

496
00:39:28,420 --> 00:39:32,400
and the future promises some amazing new materials and applications.

497
00:39:33,820 --> 00:39:38,160
Already, political candidates and others who fear assassination when publicly speaking

498
00:39:38,161 --> 00:39:43,440
can use the Instantaneous Personnel Protection Shield, designed by IBIS-TECH.

499
00:39:43,980 --> 00:39:47,680
The Instantaneous Personnel Protection System, or as we call it, IPS,

500
00:39:48,320 --> 00:39:53,380
is a high-technology integration which will detect an incoming bullet

501
00:39:53,381 --> 00:39:59,220
and actually deploy a protective blanket before the bullet can reach the target.

502
00:39:59,221 --> 00:40:09,880
The system is designed to be able to detect a bullet approaching the target in approximately

503
00:40:09,881 --> 00:40:17,900
five to ten milliseconds and then deploy in 40 to 50 milliseconds.

504
00:40:17,901 --> 00:40:23,940
Another incredible application of burgeoning safety technology is being used by NASA.

505
00:40:23,941 --> 00:40:28,680
The government is installing Kevlar-based materials around the outside of the space station

506
00:40:28,681 --> 00:40:33,760
to protect it from floating space debris, making even structures in outer space bulletproof.

507
00:40:33,840 --> 00:40:35,700
There are two areas where you're concerned.

508
00:40:36,180 --> 00:40:39,660
One is from micrometeoroid or meteoroid impacts on the spacecraft.

509
00:40:39,920 --> 00:40:45,300
The other one is actually from orbital debris, pieces of stuff we've left up there on previous missions.

510
00:40:45,480 --> 00:40:46,760
And there's a lot of it up there now.

511
00:40:46,960 --> 00:40:53,280
Closer to home, bullet-resistant clothing is another area in which ballistics technology is making great strides.

512
00:40:54,140 --> 00:40:57,320
Formerly reserved for James Bond-type spies and government officials,

513
00:40:57,940 --> 00:41:01,200
the demand for personal protection has now made it to the general public.

514
00:41:02,020 --> 00:41:05,220
At Bulletproof Me, windbreakers and coats are best sellers.

515
00:41:05,820 --> 00:41:08,600
Special orders? Dinner jackets and raincoats.

516
00:41:09,700 --> 00:41:13,940
In the future, some bullet-resistant materials could be harvested from nature.

517
00:41:13,941 --> 00:41:22,560
Another area which is being evaluated and looked at now is in the biomaterials arena.

518
00:41:22,561 --> 00:41:28,380
And this is where we're just beginning to understand what Mother Nature has produced and how strong that is.

519
00:41:28,820 --> 00:41:34,940
A very good case, which has been in study now for years by the Army, is the silk from the orb spider.

520
00:41:35,860 --> 00:41:38,420
Spider silk is five times stronger than steel.

521
00:41:39,300 --> 00:41:45,820
Weaving the silk together tightly and layering it, as is done with synthetics such as Kevlar, would create a super strong vest.

522
00:41:46,400 --> 00:41:48,600
The material does, however, have a drawback.

523
00:41:48,980 --> 00:41:50,300
It stretches too much.

524
00:41:50,920 --> 00:41:54,560
A bullet would at present penetrate too deeply into a spider silk vest.

525
00:41:55,580 --> 00:41:59,360
Harvesting spiders on a large enough scale to collect silk for bulletproof vests

526
00:41:59,361 --> 00:42:01,420
would also be difficult and expensive.

527
00:42:02,180 --> 00:42:06,020
The ability to create spider silk body armor is still in the distant future,

528
00:42:06,460 --> 00:42:09,580
but scientists are working on spin-offs inspired by nature.

529
00:42:10,600 --> 00:42:15,320
The difficulty with it, of course, is that Mother Nature knows how to produce it, and we don't.

530
00:42:15,321 --> 00:42:24,500
But this is where genetic engineering, materials engineering, and all the other sciences now start coming into play.

531
00:42:24,740 --> 00:42:29,480
In other areas, we can look at, for instance, the shell of an abalone,

532
00:42:29,720 --> 00:42:35,320
which is an amazingly very strong, very tough, very resilient material,

533
00:42:35,820 --> 00:42:39,760
something we would like to mimic in an armor material.

534
00:42:40,800 --> 00:42:43,100
With a shell only one-eighth of an inch thick,

535
00:42:43,101 --> 00:42:48,100
the abalone is able to protect itself from almost 300 pounds of pressure per square inch.

536
00:42:49,180 --> 00:42:51,960
Advances in man-made materials are equally remarkable.

537
00:42:52,640 --> 00:42:58,620
In the future, bulletproof smart materials will work by remembering what their original shape was and bouncing back into it.

538
00:42:59,520 --> 00:43:03,680
Theoretically, they would be designed with their molecular structure purposely misarranged.

539
00:43:04,400 --> 00:43:09,200
If a bullet struck the material, the heat from the shot would cause the molecules to correctly align.

540
00:43:09,940 --> 00:43:12,740
This action would instantaneously strengthen the material.

541
00:43:13,100 --> 00:43:15,100
causing it to stop the bullet.

542
00:43:15,101 --> 00:43:18,100
Smart materials are still very much off in the future,

543
00:43:18,101 --> 00:43:25,100
mainly because when a bullet impacts a piece of armor that occurs in such a very short time frame,

544
00:43:25,101 --> 00:43:31,100
it's not clear yet as though those materials will actually be able to respond in thousandths of a second.

545
00:43:31,101 --> 00:43:35,100
With such mind-boggling bulletproof possibilities on the horizon,

546
00:43:35,101 --> 00:43:39,100
it's not inconceivable that bulletproof technology will one day lead to a world

547
00:43:39,101 --> 00:43:42,960
world in which everyone and everything will be routinely bulletproofed.

548
00:43:43,400 --> 00:43:47,240
All the clothes we wear, the cars we drive, the buildings we use.

549
00:43:47,680 --> 00:43:52,500
But if history is any guide, the day the world really figures out a way to bulletproof itself

550
00:43:52,501 --> 00:43:57,580
will be the same day some new, as yet unimaginable weapon of death will emerge.

551
00:43:57,581 --> 00:44:04,120
And the whole long cycle of devising ways to protect ourselves will begin all over again.

552
00:44:04,121 --> 00:44:12,760
Imagine falling through the air at 3,000 miles per hour and landing on this small piece of metal.

553
00:44:16,500 --> 00:44:20,320
Now imagine the opposite, and that's what you get when the trigger is pulled.

554
00:44:21,040 --> 00:44:23,800
Bullets on Modern Marvels, next on the History Channel.

